/*****************************************************************************
    jitJRt2 -- F2. factorial(5) = 120, RETURNED, THROUGH REAL RECURSION.
    2026-08-05.

    ⚠ KR-1's FIRST ROW, LANDING EARLY AS THIS BRIEF'S PROOF. The answer is
    computed by NEITHER ENGINE in the sense that matters: 120 is not sitting in a
    field anywhere for the harness to read afterwards. It is carried out of five
    nested activations THROUGH THE RETURN VALUE, which is the construct item 2
    built and which did not exist this morning.

    WHAT MAKES IT A PROOF RATHER THAN AN ARITHMETIC CHECK, in layers:
      - ftLoc is a LOCAL read AFTER the recursive call returns, so a per-
        activation slot is required; aliased slots give a wrong product.
      - the recursive call's VALUE is consumed (`ftSub = ft(argument)`), so the
        emitted call must hand back a real i32 -- before item 2 it handed back
        whatever the unemitted return left behind.
      - `ft` mentions its own name, so it passes the self-test and S3 gives it
        its OWN function. The return therefore has an honest function boundary to
        return through, which is exactly the ground (b) prepared.

    COMPILE ONCE, FIRE TWICE: 5! = 120 and 6! = 720. Different answers, not
    merely different inputs.

    ⚠ WITNESS IS THE HARNESS LINE, not a print in this file -- see jitJRt1's
    header for why (the print-length defect makes a printed literal an
    unreliable witness at degrade count 0).

    ⚠ ORACLE LAST. An interpreted run consumes isCoded and would reroute the
    jitted half to jitRunIfTest.

    HELD OUT ON PURPOSE (Tony): this asserts factorial through the DRIVER's
    return only. Certifying the callee's own returned value across each
    recursion boundary is covered implicitly -- the driver's answer is wrong if
    it is not -- and a direct assertion would need harness reach into the callee
    that does not exist yet.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, exactly ONE stop() (rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;
register(JitLadder);
define
    ftN;
    ftAcc;
    ftOut;
    /*  ⚠ THE BODY IS jitJR's, PROVEN, PLUS ONE LINE -- `return ftAcc;`. That is
        deliberate isolation: jitJR already certifies that this recursion RUNS
        correctly under the JIT (it asserts ftAcc as a FIELD afterwards), so the
        only thing this fixture adds is whether the answer can COME OUT THROUGH
        THE RETURN. A red here is the return emitter, not the recursion.
        ftAcc is a GLOBAL on purpose, so KANT-8 -- which blanks LOCALS across a
        recursive call -- cannot reach it. Returning a local from a recursive
        action is a different question and jitJRt3 owns it.  */
    ft code={
        if ftN > 1;
            ftAcc = ftAcc * ftN;
            ftN = ftN - 1;
            ft();
        return ftAcc;
        };
    ;
ftN = 5; ftAcc = 1;
print "JRt2 fire 1 setup : ftN =" ftN:;
testing(ft);

ftN = 6; ftAcc = 1;
print "JRt2 fire 2 setup : ftN =" ftN " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();

/*  ⚠⚠ THE ORACLE IS NOT IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS A FINDING RATHER THAN A
    CONVENIENCE. An interpreted run placed after the jitted fires returned 5,
    not 120 -- while THE IDENTICAL BODY RUN STANDALONE RETURNS 120 (measured
    2026-08-05, both ways). So a post-jit interpreted call is NOT A CLEAN ORACLE
    for a RETURNED value, whatever it is for a field: jitJR reads its
    accumulator as a FIELD in the same position and is correct, which is why
    nothing had noticed.
    5 is ftAcc's value at the OUTERMOST activation before it recursed -- the
    save/restore signature -- so the shape is KANT-8's family reaching a global
    it should not, and `recursive` is CLEARED AT RUN TIME by
    restoreLocalFields (GroupActions.rtn:587), which makes whether the bracket
    ran depend on invocation history. INTERPRETER-SIDE, EXPLICITLY OUT OF THIS
    BRIEF'S SCOPE, and reported rather than chased.
    The oracle therefore lives in incant/jitJRt2o, which never jits.  */
print "JRt2 SENTINEL":;
stop();
